{
  "title": "Insta360 GO Ultra frame with a cold-shoe mount",
  "summary": "A TPU band that stretches over the GO Ultra in its Action Pod, a PLA sled with GoPro-style prongs inside it, and a cold-shoe foot with a lock wheel and a 3-prong head. Fits the Nikon, the rig's cold shoes or any shoe; the flip screen, buttons and USB-C stay free.",
  "status": "preview",
  "date": "2026-11-26",
  "material": "TPU (band, Gås) + PLA (sled and hardware, TNT, 0.12 mm, 6 walls, 60 % gyroid)",
  "notes": "Designed without the camera in hand, from Insta360's specs (pod + camera 70.4 x 48.8 x 33.3 mm) and outline drawings, so the first print is the fit test. Band (TPU): wraps the pod's ends and bottom 0.3 mm undersize per side so it stretches on; lips over the front edges; small caps on the two front top corners; nothing on the back (screen) and nothing on the top's back 9 mm, where the screen hinges (the complaint about the MakerWorld frame). Windows in both ends for the power and Q buttons, USB-C, release switch, speaker and lanyard hole. Sled (PLA): sits inside the band under the pod; two pegs drop into the pod's mounting-latch slots so it can't slide out backwards; two GoPro-style prongs come out through the band underneath. Mount (PLA): ISO-style shoe foot in two widths (one rear notch 18.20 mm, two notches 18.45 mm; keep the snug one) with a printed M11 x 1.5 stud, a knurled lock wheel that clamps the foot in the shoe, and a 3-prong head (same prongs, thumbscrew and nut as the Kinect rig's riser) pressed onto a square key: forward, sideways or back. Put the wheel on before the head; CA glue makes the head permanent. Check on the first fit: the band slides on snug, the pegs find the latch slots, the screen flips without touching the band, and every button and the USB-C port are in a window. Plates: TNT, PLA at 230 C / 12 mm3/s, 1 h 50 min, 29 g, bed support under the sled only. Gås, TPU, 43 min, 8 g, 4 mm brim, no supports.",
